[41.933388, "o", "le; z = zipfile.ZipFile('/tmp/tmpg4vylt_j/pip-…\r\n    ✗ bash — exit 1\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 18 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ The temp dir gets cleaned up. The issue is clear: `ensurepip` thinks pip …\r\n[approval] bash: rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info && python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info &&…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 19 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 20 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 21 tool call(s) so far\r\npip is working again. Here's what was wrong and what I did:\r\n\r\n**Problem:** The `pip` package's actual code files had been deleted from `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/`, but the `.dist-in"]
[41.933403, "o", "fo` metadata directory (`pip-24.3.1.dist-info`) was still there. This caused `pip3` to crash with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'` because the import machinery found the metadata but no actual `pip` package to import.\r\n\r\n**Fix:** I removed the stale `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` directory and re-ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade`, which restored the full pip package from the bundled wheel that ships with Python 3.13.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=qwen3.6-35b tools=21 surface=de1fadd2f308\r\n"]
